ing, you know, ultimately gets to be listed. Some of that has changed over time. Some of it seems a little bit nebulous and undefined. You see a few different ways of how people are talking about it. So it maybe go into a couple of ways that it gets talked about and then how you're talking about it with your bags. Yeah. So temperature ratings on sleeping bags are probably one of the most frustrating things for all of us that use sleeping bags because it's the wild West out there. There, there is, you can, as a, let's say a manufacturer sleeping bags, you can put whatever temperature rating you want on a sleeping bag. There's no governing body that says you can't say this is a 20 degree bag. Even if you know, as a company that like, this is not really a 20 degree bag. And unfortunately we have all been, uh, acclimatized to thinking that a 20 degree bag is really a 30 degree bag or zero degree bag is really a 15 degree bag because it feels like we've all been like lied to for so long. And, and we're accustomed to sleep, to sleeping bag temperature ratings, not being, uh, accurate in terms of, of being reliable, uh, being reliably able to keep you warm at that specific rating. Right. Um, so I just want everybody to know if you're not aware, um, there are standardized tests for testing, uh, temperature ratings, but there is no requirement that any company, uh, has to, uh, uh, like abide by a certain rule for telling you what that temperature rating is, which is why sometimes, you know, one company X and company Y both have a 20 degree bag and one is much warmer than the other. Why is that? That's because there's no rules. Um, okay. So let's talk about, I, I do want to comment. That seems pretty wild to me because depending on where you use this bag, if you're going off that number, it is a critical piece of gear that not only might dictate your comfort, but I mean, I don't want to be dramatic here, but like a life and death situation per, I don't know. Oh yeah. Because 